## Web Services Technologies

### 1. **SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol)**

**Definition**: Protocol for exchanging structured messages over HTTP

**Characteristics**:
- XML-based
- Uses WSDL (Web Services Description Language)
- Formal contract
- Heavy-weight
- WS-* standards support

**Structure**:
```xml
<soap:Envelope xmlns:soap="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/">
  <soap:Header>
    <!-- Headers (optional) -->
  </soap:Header>
  <soap:Body>
    <!-- Request/Response -->
  </soap:Body>
</soap:Envelope>
```

**Example**:
```xml
<?xml version="1.0"?>
<soap:Envelope xmlns:soap="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/">
  <soap:Body>
    <GetCustomer xmlns="http://example.com/service">
      <customerId>123</customerId>
    </GetCustomer>
  </soap:Body>
</soap:Envelope>
```

**Advantages**:
- Strongly typed
- Built-in error handling
- WS-Security, WS-Addressing support
- Language agnostic

**Disadvantages**:
- Verbose
- Complex
- Slower than REST
- Less suitable for web/mobile

### 2. **REST (Representational State Transfer)**

**Definition**: Architectural style using HTTP methods and resources

**Principles**:
- Resources (identified by URIs)
- HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
- Statelessness
- Cacheability
- Uniform interface

**Example**:
```
GET    /api/customers/123          → Retrieve customer
POST   /api/customers              → Create customer
PUT    /api/customers/123          → Update customer
DELETE /api/customers/123          → Delete customer
```

**Response**:
```json
{
  "id": 123,
  "name": "John Doe",
  "email": "john@example.com",
  "status": "active"
}
```

**Advantages**:
- Simple and lightweight
- HTTP cacheable
- Browser-friendly
- Easy to test
- Best for web/mobile

**Disadvantages**:
- No formal contract (though OpenAPI helps)
- Versioning challenges
- Limited metadata in protocol
- Error handling less standardized

### 3. **Web Services Description Language (WSDL)**

**Purpose**: XML-based language describing SOAP web services

**Structure**:
```xml
<definitions>
  <types>
    <!-- Data types (schemas) -->
  </types>
  <message>
    <!-- Input/Output messages -->
  </message>
  <portType>
    <!-- Abstract operations -->
  </portType>
  <binding>
    <!-- Protocol binding (SOAP, HTTP) -->
  </binding>
  <service>
    <!-- Service endpoints -->
  </service>
</definitions>
```

**Benefits**:
- Auto-generation of client proxies
- Clear contract
- Complete documentation
- Tool support

### 4. **UDDI (Universal Description Discovery Integration)**

**Purpose**: Service registry and discovery mechanism

**Components**:
```
UDDI Registry
├── White Pages (Who?)
│   └── Business entity info
├── Yellow Pages (What?)
│   └── Services by category
└── Green Pages (How?)
    └── Technical details
```

**Current Status**: 
- Less popular in practice
- Replaced by lightweight registries
- Still supported in enterprise environments

### 5. **OpenAPI/Swagger**

**Purpose**: Standard for describing REST APIs

**Example**:
```yaml
openapi: 3.0.0
info:
  title: Customer API
  version: 1.0.0

paths:
  /customers/{id}:
    get:
      summary: Get customer by ID
      parameters:
        - name: id
          in: path
          required: true
          schema:
            type: integer
      responses:
        '200':
          description: Customer found
          content:
            application/json:
              schema:
                $ref: '#/components/schemas/Customer'
```

**Benefits**:
- Human and machine readable
- Auto-generates documentation
- Code generation support
- Testing capabilities

